Z||000000||Hank is Here||12-04-2000||01:11 AM||||I am sick and tired right now of writing a 10 page paper on efficency right now so time to take a break.<P>So does anyone work with LED's?   I want to play around with them for general automotive applications.  It could be fun since from what I understand they are not voltage specific.  I play with old jeeps 6V and mil jeeps 24V and then you have your normal cars at 12V. it would be neat to have the same light to fit all three. And isn't the auto industry gonig to 46V by like 2006 or something? not sure how much weight saving there is by that but who knows.   <BR>Basically has anyone played with LED's, does anyone know where I can get the basic parts cheap so I can customize them?  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||PearlBlueSoul||12-04-2000||01:42 AM||||I know APC already makes 1157 (dual element) and the single element bulb replacements.  Duno bout them tho.  only thing you'd have to really make would be a circuit board for it.  <P>------------------<BR>1987 Toyota MR2 N/A 5 speed<BR>  <A HREF="http://www.atlantamr2s.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.atlantamr2s.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/darionscard</A>  <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard/Thumb.jpg">       <BR><B>Notable mods:</B>  Sus tech springs and sway bars, Toyota strut tower brace, KYB strut inserts, HKS exhaust, NGK plugs/wires, Momo Racer wheels (14x6), Custom intake, Kenwood 12" speaker in front trunk, Jensen amp.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||12-05-2000||06:47 PM||||You dont need a circuit board for just an LED. You would probally want to put a resistor on it to keep its voltage around 2-3V. Very easy to do. Looking at the LED from the top of it, there should be a little flat area on the normally round base of it, that indicates the negative(-) side. It can also be found by looking at the wires, the shortest one is the negative and the longer one is positive. You do need to put a resistor on it to keep voltage low, or they will burn up and blow up. Its always fun to do that when there is nothing else to do in electronics.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/transsig.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>"It's always in the last place you look." Of course it is. Why would you keep looking after you've found it? Do people do this? Who and where are they?</I>||||reg||1
Z||000003||RNGCRSH||12-05-2000||09:45 PM||||You can also use a Flashing LED as a driver for a blinker circuit, takes some basic electronics skills. ||||reg||1
